DATA={'IV/19': {'intro': 'The Gamma function extends factorials to the complex plane through an integral, functional equation, and analytic continuation. It is the prototype of a special function whose poles and growth encode arithmetic structure.', 'sections': [('Euler integral', 'For $\\Re s>0$, define $\\Gamma(s)=\\int_0^\\infty t^{s-1}e^{-t}dt$.'), ('Convergence', 'Near zero the condition $\\Re s>0$ controls $t^{s-1}$; at infinity exponential decay dominates every power.'), ('Functional equation', 'Integration by parts gives $\\Gamma(s+1)=s\\Gamma(s)$.'), ('Factorials', 'For positive integers, $\\Gamma(n+1)=n!$.'), ('Analyticity in the half-plane', 'Differentiation under the integral sign gives holomorphic dependence on $s$ for $\\Re s>0$.'), ('Meromorphic continuation', 'The functional equation extends $\\Gamma$ to the plane with simple poles at nonpositive integers.'), ('Residues at poles', 'Pole residues are determined recursively from the residue at zero.'), ('Logarithmic derivative', "The digamma function $\\psi=\\Gamma'/\\Gamma$ converts products and functional identities into additive formulas."), ('Growth preview', 'Stirling asymptotics describe factorial-like growth in sectors.')], 'theorems': [('Gamma functional equation', 'For $\\Re s>0$, $\\Gamma(s+1)=s\\Gamma(s)$.', 'Integrate $\\int_0^\\infty t^s e^{-t}dt$ by parts. Boundary terms vanish because $t^s e^{-t}$ decays at infinity and tends to zero at the origin under the real-part condition.'), ('Meromorphic continuation', 'The functional equation extends $\\Gamma$ meromorphically to $\\mathbb C$ with simple poles at $0,-1,-2,\\dots$.', 'For any $N$, define $\\Gamma(s)=\\Gamma(s+N)/(s(s+1)\\cdots(s+N-1))$ where the numerator integral is holomorphic for $\\Re(s+N)>0$. These local formulas agree on overlaps by the functional equation.'), ('Residue formula', 'At $s=-n$, $n\\ge0$, $\\operatorname{Res}(\\Gamma,-n)=(-1)^n/n!$.', 'Start from $\\operatorname{Res}(\\Gamma,0)=1$ using $\\Gamma(s+1)=s\\Gamma(s)$ and propagate residues recursively through the functional equation.')]}, 'IV/20': {'intro': 'The Beta function packages a two-endpoint integral and connects directly to Gamma through a change of variables. Their identities provide a compact calculus for factorials, binomial coefficients, trigonometric integrals, and parameter differentiation.', 'sections': [('Beta integral', 'For $\\Re x,\\Re y>0$, $B(x,y)=\\int_0^1 t^{x-1}(1-t)^{y-1}dt$.'), ('Symmetry', 'The substitution $t\\mapsto1-t$ gives $B(x,y)=B(y,x)$.'), ('Gamma relation', 'A two-dimensional change of variables yields $B(x,y)=\\Gamma(x)\\Gamma(y)/\\Gamma(x+y)$.'), ('Recurrences', 'Integration by parts and the Gamma relation produce rational recurrences in the parameters.'), ('Trigonometric form', 'The substitution $t=\\sin^2\\theta$ gives Beta evaluations of sine and cosine powers.'), ('Factorial identities', 'At integer and half-integer arguments, Beta and Gamma produce binomial and central-binomial formulas.'), ('Parameter derivatives', 'Differentiation with respect to parameters introduces logarithmic factors inside the integral.'), ('Duplication preview', 'Combining Beta identities yields the Gamma duplication formula.'), ('Analytic continuation', 'The Gamma quotient extends Beta meromorphically beyond its defining integral region.')], 'theorems': [('Beta--Gamma identity', 'For $\\Re x,\\Re y>0$, $B(x,y)=\\Gamma(x)\\Gamma(y)/\\Gamma(x+y)$.', 'Write the product of Gamma integrals over the first quadrant and substitute $u=r t$, $v=r(1-t)$ with $r>0$, $0<t<1$. The Jacobian is $r$, and the integral factors into $\\Gamma(x+y)B(x,y)$.'), ('Trigonometric Beta formula', 'For $a,b>0$, $\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^{a-1}\\theta\\cos^{b-1}\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\frac12B(a/2,b/2)$.', 'Set $t=\\sin^2\\theta$, so $dt=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$ and collect the resulting powers.'), ('Beta recurrence', '$B(x+1,y)=\\frac{x}{x+y}B(x,y)$ in the common domain of definition.', 'Use the Beta--Gamma identity and the Gamma functional equation in numerator and denominator.')]}, 'IV/21': {'intro': 'Keyhole contours turn branch discontinuities into computable factors. They are the standard mechanism for integrals involving fractional powers, logarithms, and branch cuts.', 'sections': [('Branch choice', 'A branch of $z^\\alpha=e^{\\alpha\\operatorname{Log} z}$ must be fixed before the contour is chosen.'), ('Keyhole geometry', 'A keyhole contour runs along the two sides of a branch cut and connects them by small and large circles.'), ('Jump factor', 'The two boundary values of $z^\\alpha$ differ by a phase $e^{2\\pi i\\alpha}$ across the cut.'), ('Vanishing circular arcs', 'Power estimates determine whether the small and large circular contributions disappear.'), ('Residue contribution', 'Poles away from the branch cut contribute through the residue theorem.'), ('Logarithmic integrals', 'Differentiating branch-power identities with respect to exponents produces logarithms.'), ('Orientation bookkeeping', 'Upper and lower sides of the cut have opposite traversal directions.'), ('Parameter windows', 'The allowed real parts of exponents are dictated by convergence at zero and infinity.'), ('Contour variants', 'Hankel contours and wedge contours implement the same jump principle for other branch geometries.')], 'theorems': [('Keyhole jump identity', 'For a branch cut along the positive real axis with argument in $(0,2\\pi)$, the upper and lower boundary values of $z^\\alpha$ differ by the factor $e^{2\\pi i\\alpha}$.', 'On the upper bank the argument tends to $0$, while on the lower bank it tends to $2\\pi$. Substitution into $e^{\\alpha(\\log r+i\\arg z)}$ gives the factor.'), ('Keyhole reduction principle', 'If the circular arcs vanish, the keyhole contour integral reduces to a scalar jump factor times the corresponding real integral along the cut.', 'Write the contour as upper bank plus outer circle plus lower bank plus inner circle. Let the radii tend to their limits; the circle contributions vanish and the two bank integrals combine using their branch-value relation and opposite orientations.'), ('Differentiation in exponent', 'When a keyhole identity is locally uniformly convergent in a parameter $\\alpha$, differentiating it with respect to $\\alpha$ introduces factors of $\\operatorname{Log} z$ and yields logarithmic integral identities.', 'Differentiate $z^\\alpha=e^{\\alpha\\operatorname{Log} z}$ under the integral sign, justified by a common integrable majorant on a compact parameter subregion.')]}, 'IV/22': {'intro': 'Analytic continuation naturally leads beyond planar single-valued functions. A Riemann surface is the geometric object on which a locally holomorphic multivalued expression becomes an ordinary single-valued holomorphic function.', 'sections': [('Local branches', 'Square roots, logarithms, and inverse functions have locally single-valued holomorphic branches.'), ('Branch incompatibility', 'Different continuations around loops can disagree after returning to the same plane point.'), ('Surface of germs', 'Collecting compatible germs over plane points separates distinct analytic branches.'), ('Projection map', 'A Riemann surface often comes with a holomorphic projection to the plane or sphere.'), ('Charts', 'Local coordinates make the surface look like open subsets of $\\mathbb C$.'), ('Holomorphic functions on surfaces', 'Holomorphicity is defined through compatible coordinate charts.'), ('Branch points', 'Projection can fail to be locally one-to-one at ramification points.'), ('Maximal analytic continuation', 'The maximal continuation of a germ can be organized as a connected Riemann surface.'), ('Examples', 'The logarithm surface and the square-root surface show infinite-sheeted and two-sheeted behavior.')], 'theorems': [('Local single-valuedness', 'Every point of a Riemann surface has a neighborhood on which a holomorphic projection that is locally biholomorphic can be used as a complex coordinate.', 'This is built into the chart structure: a local coordinate is a homeomorphism to a plane domain with holomorphic transition maps.'), ('Germ-surface principle', 'Compatible analytic continuations of a germ can be organized into a set of germs with a natural projection to the base point and a local complex coordinate given by that projection.', 'Around each germ choose a representative on a small disk. Nearby points correspond to the germs of the same representative; these neighborhoods provide charts and compatible transition maps.'), ('Single-valued lift', 'A multivalued analytic expression becomes single-valued when evaluated on its surface of analytic germs.', 'A point of the surface records not just the base coordinate but the chosen local germ, so evaluation of that germ is unambiguous.')]}, 'IV/23': {'intro': 'Covering maps formalize the idea of many sheets lying over one base domain. Path lifting and monodromy translate topology of loops into permutations of analytic branches.', 'sections': [('Covering definition', 'A covering map is locally a disjoint union of homeomorphic sheets over evenly covered neighborhoods.'), ('Sheets', 'Each component over an evenly covered neighborhood projects homeomorphically to the neighborhood.'), ('Path lifting', 'A path in the base has a unique lift once its starting point in the fiber is chosen.'), ('Loop lifting', 'A loop may lift to a path whose endpoint is a different point of the same fiber.'), ('Monodromy action', 'Homotopy classes of loops act by permutations on the fiber.'), ('Deck transformations', 'Automorphisms of the covering commute with the projection and permute sheets globally.'), ('Exponential covering', '$z\\mapsto e^z$ covers $\\mathbb C^*$ with deck translations by $2\\pi i\\mathbb Z$.'), ('Power maps', '$z\\mapsto z^n$ covers $\\mathbb C^*$ by itself with cyclic monodromy.'), ('Analytic continuation', 'Branches of inverse functions move along lifted paths, making covering theory a natural language for continuation without branch points.')], 'theorems': [('Unique path lifting', 'For a covering $p:X\\to Y$, any path $\\gamma$ in $Y$ and any $x_0\\in p^{-1}(\\gamma(0))$ have a unique lift starting at $x_0$.', 'Cover the compact parameter interval by subintervals mapping into evenly covered neighborhoods and construct the lift step by step. Local uniqueness on each sheet forces global uniqueness.'), ('Homotopy invariance of monodromy', 'Homotopic loops with fixed base point induce the same permutation of the fiber.', 'Use the homotopy lifting property for coverings. The lifted endpoints vary continuously in a discrete fiber, hence remain constant through the homotopy.'), ('Exponential covering', 'The exponential map $\\exp:\\mathbb C\\to\\mathbb C^*$ is a covering map with deck group $z\\mapsto z+2\\pi i k$.', 'Small disks in $\\mathbb C^*$ avoiding zero admit logarithm branches; their inverse images are disjoint translates of one logarithm branch, and exponential is biholomorphic on each translate.')]}, 'IV/24': {'intro': 'Branched coverings relax ordinary covering behavior at isolated ramification points. Locally they are modeled by power maps, and the ramification indices encode how sheets merge.', 'sections': [('Branched covering', 'Away from a discrete branch set, the map is an ordinary covering; over branch points sheets merge.'), ('Local power model', 'Near a ramification point, suitable coordinates often put the map in form $w=z^e$.'), ('Ramification index', 'The integer $e$ measures local multiplicity; $e>1$ means ramification.'), ('Branch values', 'Images of ramification points are branch values in the base.'), ('Square-root surface', 'The surface $w^2=z$ is a two-sheeted cover of the sphere branched at zero and infinity.'), ('Polynomial maps', 'A polynomial viewed on the sphere gives a branched covering whose critical points are ramification points.'), ('Monodromy around branch values', 'Loops around branch values permute sheets nontrivially.'), ('Degree', 'Away from branch values, every fiber has the same number of points counted with multiplicity.'), ('Riemann--Hurwitz preview', 'Total ramification contributes to the genus relation between compact surfaces.')], 'theorems': [('Local ramification model', 'A nonconstant holomorphic map between Riemann surfaces has local coordinates in which it is $z\\mapsto z^e$ for a unique integer $e\\ge1$.', 'Expand the map in a local coordinate around the source point, factor out the first nonzero term of order $e$, and absorb the nonvanishing holomorphic factor into a new local coordinate using a holomorphic $e$th root.'), ('Critical-point criterion', 'A point is ramified iff the derivative of a local coordinate representation vanishes there.', 'In the local model $z^e$, the derivative at zero is nonzero exactly when $e=1$.'), ('Constant generic degree', 'For a proper finite branched covering, the sum of ramification multiplicities in a fiber is constant away from degenerations and equals the degree.', 'Locally, zeros of the equation $p(x)=y$ are counted with multiplicity; the argument principle makes this count stable as $y$ moves without crossing branch values.')]}, 'IV/25': {'intro': 'Gluing constructs Riemann surfaces by identifying open pieces through holomorphic transition maps. This is the global counterpart of defining a manifold from compatible charts.', 'sections': [('Gluing data', 'Start with complex domains and specify identifications between open subsets.'), ('Transition maps', 'Identifications must be biholomorphic and satisfy inverse and cocycle compatibility.'), ('Quotient space', 'The disjoint union modulo the identifications becomes the candidate surface.'), ('Hausdorff condition', 'Gluing must be controlled so the quotient remains Hausdorff.'), ('Charts from pieces', 'Each original domain descends locally to a coordinate chart on the quotient.'), ('Sphere from two charts', 'Two copies of the plane glued by $w=1/z$ on punctured regions produce the Riemann sphere.'), ('Algebraic curves', 'Local branches of an algebraic relation can be glued into a smooth Riemann surface.'), ('Cut-and-paste constructions', 'Copies of slit planes or polygons can be glued to produce root surfaces and tori.'), ('Transition-cocycle viewpoint', 'The global complex structure is completely encoded by holomorphic chart transition functions.')], 'theorems': [('Holomorphic gluing theorem', 'If Hausdorff quotient gluing data have biholomorphic transition maps satisfying the cocycle conditions, the quotient inherits a unique Riemann-surface structure for which the piece maps are local charts.', 'Use the images of the original domains as charts. On overlaps their transition maps are exactly the prescribed biholomorphisms, so the atlas is compatible and defines the complex structure.'), ('Sphere gluing', 'Gluing two copies of $\\mathbb C$ by $w=1/z$ on $\\mathbb C^*$ produces a compact Riemann surface biholomorphic to $\\widehat{\\mathbb C}$.', 'Map one chart to finite complex numbers and the origin of the second chart to infinity. The transition relation is precisely the standard coordinate change at infinity.'), ('Cocycle consistency', 'For triple overlaps, transition maps must satisfy $g_{ij}\\circ g_{jk}=g_{ik}$.', 'A point represented through three charts must have the same coordinate identification independent of the path through the overlap graph; the cocycle condition is exactly that independence.')]}, 'IV/26': {'intro': 'Compactification adds missing points in a way compatible with the complex structure, while genus records the basic topological type of a compact connected Riemann surface.', 'sections': [('Adding infinity', 'The complex plane compactifies to the Riemann sphere by adding one point with coordinate $w=1/z$.'), ('Puncture filling', 'A puncture can sometimes be filled when the local complex structure extends across it.'), ('Compact algebraic curves', 'Affine algebraic curves often acquire points at infinity in their projective compactification.'), ('Genus', 'For a compact orientable surface, genus counts handles and determines Euler characteristic $\\chi=2-2g$.'), ('Sphere and torus', 'The sphere has genus zero; complex tori have genus one.'), ('Meromorphic functions', 'On compact Riemann surfaces, nonconstant holomorphic functions to $\\mathbb C$ cannot exist, but meromorphic functions correspond to holomorphic maps to the sphere.'), ('Branched-cover viewpoint', 'Maps to the sphere reveal genus through their degree and ramification.'), ('Riemann--Hurwitz formula', 'For finite branched covers, Euler characteristics differ by a total ramification correction.'), ('Compactness consequences', 'Holomorphic functions, divisors, and differential forms become globally constrained on compact surfaces.')], 'theorems': [('Holomorphic functions on compact surfaces', 'Every holomorphic function from a compact connected Riemann surface to $\\mathbb C$ is constant.', 'The absolute value attains a maximum by compactness. In a local coordinate the maximum-modulus principle forces local constancy, and connectedness propagates it globally.'), ('Riemann--Hurwitz formula', 'For a degree-$d$ holomorphic map $f:X\\to Y$ of compact Riemann surfaces, $2g_X-2=d(2g_Y-2)+\\sum_{p\\in X}(e_p-1)$.', 'Triangulate the target with branch values among vertices and lift the triangulation. Faces and edges lift $d$-fold, while vertices lose exactly the total ramification multiplicity. Comparing Euler characteristics yields the formula.'), ('Genus of a double cover', 'A degree-two cover of the sphere branched simply at $r$ points has genus $g=(r-2)/2$.', 'Apply Riemann--Hurwitz with $d=2$, target genus zero, and total ramification $r$: $2g-2=-4+r$.')]}}
